==============================================================================
Which way the pinning cuts — the accumulated asymmetry vs p/w
==============================================================================

   w = 1.0, j = 0.05;  free-phase prediction <phidot> = j^2/(2w) = 1.2500e-03

     p/w        <phidot>      / free-phase      state
    0.0e+00   +1.3033e-03       1.0427       FREE — asymmetry accumulates
    1.0e-04   +1.2167e-03       0.9733       FREE — asymmetry accumulates
    1.0e-03   +5.6777e-04       0.4542       TRAPPED — nothing accumulates
    1.0e-02   +4.1235e-06       0.0033       TRAPPED — nothing accumulates
    1.0e-01   -2.0157e-15      -0.0000       TRAPPED — nothing accumulates
    1.0e+00   +4.9883e-17       0.0000       TRAPPED — nothing accumulates

   the physical point:
     pinning rate        p = m1^2/Gamma_phi = 9.375e-16 eV
     junction rate       j (set by the need) = 5.970e-03 eV
     trapping threshold  j^2/(2*thetadot)    = 2.985e-07 eV
     p / threshold = 3.14e-09
   -- the pinning sits NINE orders below the level at which it
      would begin destroying the asymmetry. Free, with margin.

VERDICT:
   THE PINNING IS THE OFF SWITCH, NOT THE PREFACTOR. Where the
   Majorana term is strong enough to hold the phase, the
   accumulated asymmetry collapses to zero: the junction still
   passes current, but the L-violating channel destroys exactly
   what arrives, which is what a trapped phase means. The
   transfer survives in this model only because the pinning is
   seventeen orders too weak to hold anything. Crediting the
   rectification to m1 has the causality backwards.
